## Problem

`FetchChronyStatus` in the OPNsense API client deliberately tolerates failures when fetching the chrony sources and sourcestats sub-endpoints: if either call errors (for anything other than a 404, which means the plugin is absent), the error is only warn-logged and the function proceeds with `status.Sources` / `status.SourceStats` left as their zero value (`nil`), ultimately returning a `nil` error overall.

The chrony collector then unconditionally emits `opnsense_chrony_sources_total` as `float64(len(data.Sources))` with no gating flag to indicate whether the sources fetch actually succeeded. Every other per-field metric in this collector (e.g. `LastRx`, `Offset`, `StdDev`) is guarded by an explicit `Has*` presence flag, but `sources_total` and the per-source metrics have no equivalent guard. As a result, a transient sub-fetch failure (e.g. a configd timeout or an HTTP 500 from the chrony controller) produces exactly the same exported value — `0` — as chronyd genuinely having lost all of its NTP sources. The collector's overall `Update()` call still returns `nil`, so `scrape_collector_success` for chrony stays `1` and gives no indication that partial data is missing.

## Evidence

`opnsense/chrony.go:268-288`:
```go
// Sources — tolerate failure; keep partial data.
sourcesURL, ok := c.endpoints["chronySources"]
if ok {
    var sourcesEnv chronyResponseEnvelope
    if err := c.do("GET", sourcesURL, nil, &sourcesEnv); err != nil {
        c.log.Warn("failed to fetch chrony sources", "err", err)
    } else {
        status.Sources = parseChronySources(sourcesEnv.Response.String())
    }
}

// SourceStats — tolerate failure; keep partial data.
sourceStatsURL, ok := c.endpoints["chronySourceStats"]
if ok {
    var statsEnv chronyResponseEnvelope
    if err := c.do("GET", sourceStatsURL, nil, &statsEnv); err != nil {
        c.log.Warn("failed to fetch chrony sourcestats", "err", err)
    } else {
        status.SourceStats = parseChronySourceStats(statsEnv.Response.String())
    }
}

return status, nil
```

`internal/collector/chrony.go:156-158`:
```go
// Sources total + per-source metrics.
ch <- prometheus.MustNewConstMetric(c.sourcesTotal, prometheus.GaugeValue,
    float64(len(data.Sources)), c.instance)
```

`opnsense/chrony_test.go` has no test covering the non-404-error path for `chronySources`/`chronySourceStats` — only the success and 404-plugin-absent paths are tested. This finding is not livebox-validated (the failure path requires simulating a transient 500/timeout, which was verified by code inspection only).

## Failure scenario / impact

A configd hiccup makes `api/chrony/service/chronysources` return a transient 500 for one scrape while the tracking call still succeeds. `opnsense_chrony_sources_total` drops from its normal value (e.g. 4) to 0 for that scrape interval, and no per-source metrics are emitted. Any alert rule watching for "chrony lost all NTP sources" fires, and dashboards show a false full outage — even though chronyd itself is healthy and `endpoint_errors_total` (not typically an alerting target for this condition) is the only signal that something actually failed.

## Suggested direction

Track `HasSources`/`HasSourceStats` boolean flags on `ChronyStatus`, mirroring the existing `HasLastRx`/`HasOffset`/`HasStdDev` pattern, and have the collector skip emitting `sources_total` and per-source metrics entirely when the corresponding sub-fetch failed, rather than emitting a zero-filled value indistinguishable from a real empty state.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `ChronyStatus` (or equivalent) carries a presence/success flag for the sources fetch and for the sourcestats fetch, distinct from "zero sources present"
- [ ] `internal/collector/chrony.go` does not emit `opnsense_chrony_sources_total` or any per-source metric when the sources sub-fetch failed
- [ ] A unit test in `opnsense/chrony_test.go` covers a non-404 error from `chronySources` (and separately `chronySourceStats`) and asserts the failure is distinguishable from a genuine zero-sources response
- [ ] A unit test in the collector package asserts that `opnsense_chrony_sources_total` is not emitted (or is otherwise clearly flagged) on a sources-fetch failure
- [ ] Existing success-path and 404-plugin-absent-path tests for chrony continue to pass unchanged

Part of the production-readiness bug bash — tracker: #72

